### In-Memory Cache Implementation

An in-memory cache is a simple and fast solution, but it will be cleared when the page is refreshed. If you need persistence across sessions, you can use `localStorage`.

#### In-Memory Cache

```javascript
const weatherCache = new Map();

const fetchWeatherData = async (cityName) => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=${cityName}&units=metric&appid=YOUR_API_KEY`);
    const data = await response.json();
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching weather data:', error);
    return null;
  }
};

const getWeatherData = async (cityName) => {
  if (weatherCache.has(cityName)) {
    console.log('Returning cached data for', cityName);
    return weatherCache.get(cityName);
  }

  const data = await fetchWeatherData(cityName);
  if (data) {
    weatherCache.set(cityName, data);
  }
  return data;
};

// Example usage
getWeatherData('London').then(data => {
  console.log('Weather data:', data);
}).catch(error => {
  console.error('Error fetching weather data:', error);
});
```

